---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-1-21-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `Row 1 Seat 1
Row 1 Seat 2
Row 1 Seat 3
Row 2 Seat 1
...`;
const Figure2 = `for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
  ellipse(col * 70 + 50, 100, 40, 40);
}`;
const Figure3 = `for (let row = 0; row < 4; row++) {
  for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
    ellipse(col * 70 + 50, row * 70 + 50, 40, 40);
  }
}`;
const Figure4 = `x = col * spacing + margin
y = row * spacing + margin`;
const Figure5 = `for (let row = 0; row < 4; row++) {
  for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
    if ((row + col) % 2 === 0) {
      fill(240);
    } else {
      fill(40);
    }
    rect(col * 60, row * 60, 60, 60);
  }
}`;
const Task1 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();

  fill(120, 200, 255);

  for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
    ellipse(col * 70 + 50, 60, 40, 40);
  }
}
`;
const Task2 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();

  fill(120, 200, 255);

  for (let row = 0; row < 5; row++) {
    for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
      ellipse(col * 70 + 50, row * 70 + 50, 40, 40);
    }
  }
}
`;
const Task3 = `function setup() {
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();

  for (let row = 0; row < 5; row++) {
    for (let col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
      // Choose a fill color before drawing each circle:
      //   make it depend on row, col, or both
      //   one good option: (row + col) % 2 for a checkerboard
      //   another: use col * 50 as the red amount for a gradient

      ellipse(col * 70 + 50, row * 70 + 50, 40, 40);
    }
  }
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 1.21: p5 Lab 5 &mdash; Grids</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A dark canvas filled with an evenly spaced five by five grid of white circles" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> a loop inside a loop will place every circle in this grid. You will not type a single coordinate.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 1.21</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">p5 Lab 5 &mdash; Grids</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>A Grid Is a Nested Loop</p>
              <p>Turning Counters Into Coordinates</p>
              <p>Color From Position</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Yesterday's Output</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Twelve correct lines that look nothing like the grid they
                describe. You had to picture it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Today You Will Not Have To</h2>
              <p>
                Nested loops are the clearest thing in this course on a canvas
                and one of the murkiest in a terminal. Same code, and suddenly
                you can just see whether it is right.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>One Row</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                <CodeSnippet code="col" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> counts 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 &mdash; and{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="col * 70" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> turns that into 0, 70, 140, 210, 280.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Add a Second Loop</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                Twenty circles. The outer loop chooses the row, the inner one
                walks across it &mdash; exactly the rows-and-seats behavior from
                Activity 1.20.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Formula</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="none"
                lang="txt"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <div class="text-start mt-3">
                <ul>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <b>spacing</b> is the gap from one to the next
                  </li>
                  <li class="fragment">
                    <b>margin</b> pushes everything away from the edge
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <p class="fragment">
                Learn this shape. Every grid you ever draw uses it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why Start at Zero</h2>
              <p>
                Counting from 0 makes the first item land at{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="0 * 70" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, which is 0 &mdash; exactly where you want
                it.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Start at 1 and everything shifts over by one whole gap. This is
                the first place where Activity 1.18's{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="i = 0; i < n" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> habit pays for itself.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Color From Position</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                A checkerboard, from one conditional and the remainder operator.
                The loop counters are <i>data</i>, not just counters.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Drawing a row of shapes with one loop</li>
                <li>Drawing a grid with nested loops</li>
                <li>Converting a counter into a coordinate</li>
                <li>Choosing a color from a shape's position</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Spacing</dt>
                  <dd>The distance between neighboring items in a grid.</dd>
                  <dt>Margin</dt>
                  <dd>The offset that keeps a grid away from the canvas edge.</dd>
                  <dt>
                    <CodeSnippet code="noStroke()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
                  </dt>
                  <dd>Turns off shape outlines.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js</b> sketch named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">1-21-grid</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Back to <CodeSnippet code="setup()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> only &mdash; nothing moves today, so
            nothing needs to repeat every frame.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: One Row</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it. Five circles across the top.</li>
            <li>
              Change the <CodeSnippet code="70" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> to 40, then to 100. Watch the spacing
              open and close.
            </li>
            <li>
              Change <CodeSnippet code="col = 0" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> to{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="col = 1" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and see the whole row shift right. Put it
              back.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 5 — Grids"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: A Full Grid</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Wrap your row loop in a second loop so it repeats down the canvas.
            </li>
            <li>
              Twenty-five circles from four lines of code. Writing those by hand
              would be twenty-five <CodeSnippet code="ellipse()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> calls.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Try this:</b> swap <CodeSnippet code="row" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="col" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> in the ellipse line only. The grid looks the same
              &mdash; work out why, and why it would not if the grid were not
              square.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 5 — Grids"
            ins={{ range: "8-12" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Color It</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The single <CodeSnippet code="fill()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> has been removed from the top. Each
              circle now needs its own color, chosen inside the loops.
            </li>
            <li>
              The comments suggest two approaches. Pick one, or invent your own
              &mdash; anything where the color depends on the position counts.
            </li>
            <li>
              The <CodeSnippet code="fill()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> must come <b>before</b> the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="ellipse()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, or it colors the next circle instead of
              this one.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="p5 Lab 5 — Grids"
            ins={{ range: "8-11" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Make the Grid Adjustable</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add variables at the top for how many columns, how many rows, and
              the spacing.
            </li>
            <li>
              Work the spacing out from the canvas size and the count, so the
              grid always fills the canvas neatly however many you ask for.
            </li>
            <li>
              Test at 3 by 3 and at 10 by 10. Both should look deliberate, not
              cramped or lost in a corner.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Your finished sketch should show all of the following.</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A 5 by 5 grid of circles, evenly spaced, none touching the edge.</li>
            <li>Each circle's color decided by its row, its column, or both.</li>
            <li>
              A visible pattern &mdash; a checkerboard, a gradient, stripes.
              Something you can point at and explain from the code.
            </li>
            <li>
              No circle drawn twice and none missing. Count them: there should be
              exactly 25.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            You should be able to say which loop controls left-to-right and which
            controls top-to-bottom, without guessing.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Activity 1.20 printed rows and seats as text; today drew them as a
              grid. Which made it easier to tell whether the loops were right,
              and why?
            </li>
            <li>
              Explain what <code>col * 70 + 50</code> does, naming what the 70
              and the 50 each control.
            </li>
            <li>
              Your color depended on the loop counters. What does that tell you
              about what a counter really is?
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
